<page>
  <paragraphs>
      <title>Graphing</title>
      <p>
        We're going to start this course by talking about graphing. There are many different types of graph, but the one we want to talk about in this class is the kind the allows us to compare two quantities. We want to see how the second quantity changes when the first is modified. For example:
      </p>
      <p workspace="2in" component="b">
        <nbsp />
      </p>
      <p component="a">
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Attendance at Tigers games over the course of the season.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              United States population as time changes.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Amount of money in a bank/savings/investment account over time.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
      <p>
        In order to visualize these things, we will need a two-dimensional image, where one direction corresponds to each quantity.
      </p>
      <p workspace="2in" component="b">
        <nbsp />
      </p>
      <p component="a">
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> axes. The <m>x</m>-axis is the horizontal line and the <m>y</m>-axis is the vertical line.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              The origin, which we will label with coordinates <m>(0,0)</m>, is the point where these axes meet.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Each point gets labeled with an ordered pair <m>(a,b)</m>, where <m>a</m> indicates where the point is in the horizontal direction and <m>b</m> is where it is in the vertical direction. We call these the <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> coordinates respectively.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              We end up with four quadrants here, which we label in a counter-clockwise direction.
              <ul>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Quadrant 1 is where both the <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> coordinates are positive. 
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Quadrant 2 is where both the <m>x</m> coordinate is negative and <m>y</m> coordinate is positive. 
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Quadrant 3 is where both the <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> coordinates are negative. 
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Quadrant 4 is where both the <m>x</m> coordinate is positive and <m>y</m> coordinate is negative. 
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ul> 
              All of this together is called the <em>Cartesian plane</em> or <em>Cartesian coordinate system</em> after Rene Descartes.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
      </paragraphs>
    </page>
    <page>
      <example workspace="3in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Plot and label the points <m>(2,1)</m>, <m>(-4,3)</m>, <m>(0,0)</m>, <m>(-3,-2)</m>, <m>(3,-4)</m>, and <m>(2,-1)</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution component="a">
          <p>
            Graph
          </p>
        </solution>
      </example>
</page>
<page>
    <paragraphs>
      <title>Solutions of Equations</title>
      <p workspace="1in" component="b">
        Now, what do we want to graph? Well, we're going to want to get graphs for mathematical equations (and later, functions). First, we need to look at solutions to these equations. If I have an equation in two variables <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>, then a <em>solution</em> is
      </p>
      <p component="a">
        Now, what do we want to graph? Well, we're going to want to get graphs for mathematical equations (and later, functions). First, we need to look at solutions to these equations. If I have an equation in two variables <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>, then a <em>solution</em> is any ordered pair <m>(x,y)</m> where plugging these values into the equation results in a true statement. We will plug the first value in the ordered pair in for <m>x</m> and the second value in for <m>y</m>.
      </p>

      <example workspace="2in">
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Determine whether each ordered pair is a solution of the equation <m>3x - 2y = 5</m>.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>(1, 4)</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <solution component="a">
            <p>
              No, this is not a solution. If we plug in the values we get <m>3(1) - 2(4) = 3 - 8 = -5</m> which is not the same as the <m>5</m> on the other side of the equation. 
            </p>
          </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>(3,2)</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <solution component="a">
            <p>
              Yes, this is a solution. If we plug in the values, we get <m>3(3) - 2(2) = 9 - 4 = 5</m> which matches the other side of the equation.
            </p>
          </solution>
        </task>
      </example>
      </paragraphs>

    </page>
<page>
    <paragraphs>
      <title>Graphs of Equations</title>
      

      <p workspace="3in" component = "b">
        What is a graph of an equation?
      </p>
      <p component="a">
        What is a graph of an equation?
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              A visual representation of the solutions to the equation.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Mark a point (include it in the graph) if the value <m>(x,y)</m> solves the given equation.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
      <p>
        Graphs of equations that look like <m>Ax+By = C</m> are straight lines. Since it only takes two points to determine a straight line, we can draw these graphs by finding any two points. Two convenient points we can often use are the <em>intercepts</em>.
      </p>
      <definition xml:id="def-intercepts">
        <statement>
          <p>
            The intercepts of a graph are the places where the graph crosses the axes.
          </p>
          <p workspace="3in" component="b">
            <nbsp />
          </p>
          <p component = "a">
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p>
                  The <em>x-intercept</em> is where the graph crosses the <m>x</m>-axis. This means it must be a point of the form <m>(a,0)</m>. To find <m>a</m>, plug in zero for <m>y</m> and solve for <m>x</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  The <em>y-intercept</em> is where the graph crosses the <m>y</m>-axis. This means it must be a point of the form <m>(0,b)</m>. To find <m>b</m>, plug in zero for <m>x</m> and solve for <m>y</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </definition>
          </paragraphs>
        </page>
        <page>
    <example workspace="3in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Graph <m>3x - 2y = 5</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="a">
        <p>
          The intercepts are at (0,6) and (9,0). Can also check that (3,2) from before is a solution and is on this line.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

  </page>
  <page>
<p>
  Another way to graph equations of lines is to solve for <m>y</m> first, then plug points into <m>x</m>. In the normal way we will set up these equations in the future, we'll be looking to analyze how <m>y</m> changes when we plug in different values of <m>x</m>, so solving for <m>y</m> is usually the way to go.
</p>

<example workspace="3in">
<statement>
<p>
  Graph <m>5x + 3y = 18</m>.
</p>
  </statement>
  <solution component="a">
    <p>
      Solving for <m>y</m> gives <m>y = -\frac{5}{3}x + 6</m>. Now we can plug numbers in for <m>x</m>, and we want to do so in ways that will make this easy for us. So, we can plug in <m>0</m>, which gives <m>y=6</m>. Now, if we want to avoid fractions, we should plug in something that is a multiple of <m>3</m> for <m>x</m>. Plugging in <m>x=3</m> gives <m>y=1</m>, so we can draw our line.
    </p>
  </solution>
</example>
</page>
<page>

<p>
When we solve out for <m>y</m> like this, we are indicating that the value of <m>y</m> depends on <m>x</m>, so that <m>y</m> is the <em>dependent variable</em> and <m>x</m> is the <em>independent variable</em>.
  </p>

  <example workspace="5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Graph <m>y=x^2  - 3x + 1</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="a">
      <p>
        This equation is not a straight line. That <m>x^2</m> in there means that we can't just plot two points and draw this graph. So, we need to compute a lot of points and draw the line between them the complete the graph. Plugging in points, we get
      </p>
      <table>
        <title>Points For the Example</title>
        <tabular>
          <row header="yes">
            <cell><m>x</m></cell><cell><m>y</m></cell><cell><m>(x,y)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>-2</m></cell><cell><m>11</m></cell><cell><m>(-2,10)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>-1</m></cell><cell><m>5</m></cell><cell><m>(-1,7)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>0</m></cell><cell><m>1</m></cell><cell><m>(0,1)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>1</m></cell><cell><m>-1</m></cell><cell><m>(1, -1)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>2</m></cell><cell><m>-1</m></cell><cell><m>(2, -1)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>3</m></cell><cell><m>1</m></cell><cell><m>(3, 1)</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
      <p> And from that, we can sketch the graph. </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <p>
    Proceed to Worksheet 01 on Graphing Equations.
  </p>
  </page>
  </handout>


  <handout component = "cur">
    <title>Wednesday 9/2</title>
<objectives>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <p>
        Understand the definition of functions and relations, and determine whether a relation is a function.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Determine the domain and range of a function from points, formula definition, or graphs.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>
</objectives>

<page>
  <paragraphs>
    <title>Functions</title>
    <p>
      Assume that a university does not use a flat-rate tuition system. At this university, tuition is $2000 per semester with an additional $400 charged per credit hour.
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            How much does it cost to go there and take 1 credit hour?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            What about 5 credit hours?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            What about 18 credit hours?
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p workspace="3in">
      This is an example of a <term>function</term>.
    </p>

  </paragraphs>
</page> 
<page>

<definition xml:id="def-function">
  <statement>
    <p component="b" workspace="2in">
      A <term>function</term> is 
    </p>
  </statement>
</definition>

<p>
  Before we see some examples of functions, it will be worthwhile to define a related concept first.
</p>

<definition xml:id="def-relation">
  <statement>
    <p component="b" workspace="2in">
      A <term>relation</term> is 
    </p>
  </statement>
</definition>

<p component="b" workspace="2in">
  How do these definitions compare?
</p>
</page>
<page>
  <example>
  <statement>
    <p workspace="4in">
      Determine if the following diagrams correspond to functions.
    </p>
  </statement>  
  </example>
</page>
<page>
  <example>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Determine if each of the following correspondences are functions.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li> <title>Domain</title>
              <p>
               Students at Wayne State.    
              </p>
            </li>
            <li> <title>Range</title>
              <p>
               All AccessIDs.    
              </p>
            </li>
            <li> <title>Correspondence</title>
              <p>
               The AccessID for that student.   
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li> <title>Domain</title>
              <p>
               Mayoral election years in Detroit.    
              </p>
            </li>
            <li> <title>Range</title>
              <p>
               All Mayors of Detroit.   
              </p>
            </li>
            <li> <title>Correspondence</title>
              <p>
               The person who was elected that year. 
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li> <title>Domain</title>
              <p>
               NBA Teams who have won the national title.   
              </p>
            </li>
            <li> <title>Range</title>
              <p>
               Years    
              </p>
            </li>
            <li> <title>Correspondence</title>
              <p>
               The year each team won.  
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li> <title>Domain</title>
              <p>
               Years.   
              </p>
            </li>
            <li> <title>Range</title>
              <p>
               NBA Teams. 
              </p>
            </li>
            <li> <title>Correspondence</title>
              <p>
               The team that won the championship that year.  
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

  </example>
</page>
<page>
  <p>
    Another way to view relations (and functions) is as ordered pairs. So, the ordered pair <m>(1, 4)</m> would mean that the element <m>1</m> in the domain corresponds to <m>4</m> in the range.
  </p>
  <example workspace="1in">
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Are each of the following relations, given as sets of ordered pars, functions? Explain why or why not. For each, determine the domain and range.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>{(1, 3), (2, 4), (1, 5), (3, 1), (7, 2)}</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>{(-2, 3), (0, 6), (1, 3), (2, 0), (3, -2)}</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>{(1, 4), (2, 4), (6, 2), (5, 4)}</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </example>
</page>
<page>
  <paragraphs>
    <title>Notation for Functions</title>
    <p workspace="2in">
      The main way you want to think about functions is as machines.
    </p>
    <p workspace="2in">
      For example, if we have an equation <m>y = 2x+4</m>, we have
    </p>
    <p workspace="3in">
      We want to give the function a name so that we can refer to it. Let's call it <m>f</m>, which is the most common name used for functions. Then, instead of writing <m>y = 2x+4</m>, we can write <md>
        f(x) = 2x+4
      </md>
    </p>
  </paragraphs>
</page>
<page>
  <example workspace="1in">
    <introduction>
      <p>
        For the function <m>f(x) = 3x^2 - 2x + 1</m>, compute
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>f(1)</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>f(4)</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>f(2a)</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>f(a-1)</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </example>
</page>
<page>
  <paragraphs>
    <title>Graphs of Functions</title>
    <p workspace="1in">
      If we have a function, we can draw it's graph.
    </p>
    <example workspace="2in">
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Draw the graph of each of the following functions.
        </p>
      </introduction>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>f(x) = 6 - x^2</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>f(x) = x^3 - 4x</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>f(x) = \sqrt{x-1}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </example>

  </paragraphs>
</page>
<page>
  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        From the graph given below, determine <m>f(1)</m> and <m>f(-2)</m>.
      </p>
      <image width="60%">
    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="fun-graph-identify">
    <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
  <definition> f(x)=1/2*(-x^4 + 3*x^2 - 4)+8 </definition>
  <coordinates bbox="[-5,-2,5,9]">
    <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
    <graph function="f" domain="[-5,5]"/>
  </coordinates>
</diagram>
    </prefigure>
</image>
    </statement>
  </example>
</page>
<page>
  <paragraphs>
    <title>The Vertical Line Test</title>
    <p workspace="1in">
      We can also draw the graph of any relation; we just plot all of the ordered pairs of the correspondence. If we do this, how do we know if the graph is that of a function?
    </p>

